> 
> Adaptation investments must be tailored to specific contexts but offer significant benefits on a national scale, supporting national economic objectives and enhancing fiscal health. Adaptation investments have the potential to yield significant benefits and contribute positively to national economic objectives and fiscal health. However, these investments need to be tailored to specific contexts to realise their full potential. The diverse nature of climate impacts and socioeconomic conditions across different regions necessitates customised adaptation strategies that consider local needs and priorities.
> 
> Investing in resilience can address systemic risks and help achieve national development plans. Through strategic investments in resilience, we not only address immediate threats but also foster economic stability and sustainability, aligning closely with national economic goals and enhancing fiscal resilience against future climate-related risks.
> Investing in adaptation
> The metrics above, such as benefit-cost ratios, combine costs and benefits to consider the net effect. Here we offer some insights from literature around the costs in particular. Understanding the cost dynamics of adaptation investments involves distinguishing between capital expenditures (CapEx) and operational expenditures (OpEx). While CapEx refers to upfront investment costs, OpEx embodies the ongoing operational maintenance required to maximize lifespan and efficacy. 
> 
> Non-monetary costs, such as social and cultural impacts, must be acknowledged and integrated to present a holistic evaluation of adaptation strategies. Of the 28 studies reviewed, only 4 included residual damages after implementing adaptation strategies as a cost in addition to financial costs. Moreover, the quantification of labor reallocation, both as an adaptation strategy and as a consequence of adaptation, is limited. This gap is significant given that labour reallocation represents a common household adaptation strategy (World Bank, 2024a).
> 
> Adaptation costs are estimated at US$215 billion to US$387 billion for 2030 for developing countries (UNEP, 2023). Adaptation costs are estimated at US$320 to 365 billion/year by 2035 for developing countries (UNEP, 2025). Adaptation costs are expected to triple for the median country by 2035 (Chapagain et al. 2020). While low-income and lower-middle income countries have lower absolute adaptation costs than upper-middle and high-income countries, their adaptation finance needs constitute a higher share of GDP (WRI, 2025). Agrawala et al. (2010) finds the same results when comparing Sub-Saharan Africa and India/Indian subcontinent in comparison to China and the USA.
> 
> Adaptation requires public planning. Since the most effective adaptation strategies are public goods, their associated costs cannot be assumed by individual firms or households. The adaptation ratio of public goods such as infrastructure, effective health systems and piped water is 0.64, which is the highest out of all the strategies considered (World Bank, 2024a). Furthermore, adaptation costs are projected to rise as a percentage of GDP over time, especially for capital-intensive investments, making early investments with long-term benefits a crucial component of adaptation strategies (Agrawala et al., 2010).
> 
> Adaptation costs are lower when there is more mitigation at a worldwide level. By 2050, adaptation costs for developing countries will be 0.27-0.89% of GDP in a high-emission scenario, while in a low-emission scenario they will be 0.11-0.35% of GDP (Chapagain et al., 2020). By 2100, adaptation costs in the below 2°C scenario represent less than 1% of global GDP, whereas in a 3°C scenario they reach 4% (World Economic Forum, 2024).
> 
> Research gaps
> Evidence on adaptation is global, but large countries (US, China, India, Brazil) have vastly more estimates.
